      "section_id": "mallet_section_1",
      "section_title": "Mallet",
      "section_order": 1,
      "subsections": [
        {
          "subsection_id": "mallet_subsection_1",
          "subsection_title": "Overview",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "mallet_group_1",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/mallet_01.webp",
                  "caption": null
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "mallet_subsection_2",
          "subsection_title": "1. Introduction",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "mallet_group_2",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A surgical mallet is a hand-held instrument used to deliver controlled impact force to another surgical instrument, such as an osteotome, chisel, or bone punch, to cut, divide, shape, or remove bone."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It is commonly used in orthopaedic surgery, neurosurgery, oral and maxillofacial surgery, and selected reconstructive procedures."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "OSCE identification statement:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "“This is a surgical mallet, an instrument used to deliver controlled blows to instruments such as osteotomes and chisels during procedures involving bone cutting, bone shaping, or bone removal.”",
                  "style": "quote"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "mallet_subsection_3",
          "subsection_title": "2. Labelling and identification",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "mallet_group_3",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/mallet_02.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The typical parts of a surgical mallet are:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Label",
                    "Component",
                    "Function"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "A",
                      "Striking head",
                      "Provides the mass that generates impact force"
                    ],
                    [
                      "B",
                      "Striking face",
                      "Contacts the handle or striking end of another instrument"
                    ],
                    [
                      "C",
                      "Opposite striking face",
                      "Provides a second impact surface where the design permits"
                    ],
                    [
                      "D",
                      "Neck",
                      "Connects the head to the handle"
                    ],
                    [
                      "E",
                      "Handle",
                      "Allows the surgeon to grip and control the mallet"
                    ],
                    [
                      "F",
                      "Handle end or grip",
                      "Helps maintain secure control during use"
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "OSCE tip: Some mallets have a metal head; others have nylon, polymer, or other purpose-designed striking surfaces. Identify the actual material and design rather than assuming every mallet is entirely metal."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "mallet_subsection_4",
          "subsection_title": "3. Properties and construction",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "mallet_group_4",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "title": "A surgical mallet generally has the following properties:",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Head: Solid or weighted to provide the required impact force.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Striking face: Designed to withstand repeated blows.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Handle: Provides grip, balance, and control.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Material: May be stainless steel, another metal, nylon, polymer, or a combination of materials.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Balance: Designed to permit controlled striking.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Durability: Must withstand repeated impacts without loosening, cracking, or deforming.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Sterilization: Reusable models must be cleaned and sterilized according to the manufacturer's instructions.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The correct head material depends on the intended application. For example, a polymer-faced mallet may be chosen when a less damaging striking surface is required."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "mallet_subsection_5",
          "subsection_title": "4. Classification and types",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "mallet_group_5",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/mallet_03.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "1. Metal-headed surgical mallet"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Has a metal striking head and is used to deliver impact to suitable surgical instruments, including osteotomes and chisels."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/mallet_04.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "2. Nylon- or polymer-faced mallet"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Has a non-metallic striking surface. Used in applications where the instrument design calls for a less damaging contact surface."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/mallet_05.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "3. Orthopaedic mallet"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Used with appropriate orthopaedic instruments for controlled bone cutting, shaping, and impaction."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/mallet_06.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "4. Dental or maxillofacial mallet"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Used with compatible chisels or osteotomes during selected oral and maxillofacial procedures."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Mallets may also be classified by weight, head shape, face diameter, handle length, and intended surgical specialty."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "mallet_subsection_6",
          "subsection_title": "5. Principle of operation",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "mallet_group_6",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The surgical mallet works through the transfer of kinetic energy from the moving mallet head to the instrument being struck."
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "title": "When the surgeon swings the mallet:",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "The head gains kinetic energy.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "1."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "The striking face contacts the compatible instrument.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "2."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "The impact transfers force to that instrument.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "3."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "The instrument transmits the force to bone or another intended target.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "4."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Repeated, controlled blows produce the required cutting, shaping, or impaction effect.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "5."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The impact depends on the mass and velocity of the mallet head, as well as the contact characteristics and the receiving instrument."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Key principle: Controlled, accurately directed impact is more important than simply applying maximum force."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "mallet_subsection_7",
          "subsection_title": "6. Uses and indications",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "mallet_group_7",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "title": "A surgical mallet is used to:",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Drive an osteotome or chisel through bone.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Assist in bone division or shaping.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Advance a compatible bone punch.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Assist with selected orthopaedic impaction procedures.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Help seat certain surgical instruments or components when specifically designed for impact.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Deliver controlled force during selected reconstructive procedures.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "mallet_group_8",
              "group_title": "Surgeries in which it is used",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Specialty",
                    "Example procedure",
                    "Purpose"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Orthopaedics",
                      "Osteotomy",
                      "Drive an osteotome or chisel to divide bone"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Orthopaedics",
                      "Selected joint reconstruction procedures",
                      "Assist with compatible instrument impaction"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Neurosurgery",
                      "Selected cranial bone procedures",
                      "Deliver controlled blows to compatible bone instruments"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Oral and maxillofacial surgery",
                      "Selected osteotomies",
                      "Drive an osteotome or chisel"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Reconstructive surgery",
                      "Selected bone-shaping procedures",
                      "Apply controlled impact through a suitable instrument"
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Important distinction: The mallet usually does not cut bone directly. It supplies the impact force to the instrument that performs the cutting or shaping."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "mallet_subsection_8",
          "subsection_title": "7. Advantages",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "mallet_group_9",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Controlled impact: Allows force to be delivered in discrete blows.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "1."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Mechanical efficiency: Transfers force to an osteotome or chisel without requiring continuous pressure.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "2."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Versatility: Can be used with multiple compatible instruments.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "3."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Precision: Small, accurately directed blows can permit gradual bone division.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "4."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Durability: Appropriately maintained reusable mallets can withstand repeated use.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "5."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Specialized striking surfaces: Different head materials support different surgical requirements.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "6."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "mallet_subsection_9",
          "subsection_title": "8. Disadvantages",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "mallet_group_10",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Poorly directed blows can cause unintended injury.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Excessive force can fracture bone beyond the intended site.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Repeated impacts may damage the striking surface or the receiving instrument.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "A loose head or damaged handle may make the instrument unsafe.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Metal-on-metal impact may generate fragments if either surface is damaged.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Requires appropriate hand-eye coordination and controlled technique.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Some procedures require powered instruments or other tools rather than manual impact.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "mallet_subsection_10",
          "subsection_title": "9. Complications and risks",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "mallet_group_11",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Complication",
                    "Explanation"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Unintended fracture",
                      "Excessive or poorly directed impact may fracture adjacent or underlying bone"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Soft-tissue injury",
                      "A missed strike may injure nearby tissue"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Neurovascular injury",
                      "Impact transmitted through bone may damage nearby nerves or vessels"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Instrument damage",
                      "Excessive force or incompatible striking surfaces may deform or damage the receiving instrument"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Metal or polymer debris",
                      "Damaged striking surfaces may release particles"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Loss of control",
                      "A slippery handle or poor grip may cause a missed strike"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Excessive bone displacement",
                      "Repeated or forceful impacts may displace bone fragments"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Hearing exposure",
                      "Repeated sharp impacts can contribute to occupational noise exposure"
                    ]
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "mallet_group_12",
              "group_title": "Prevention of complications",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Inspect the mallet head, striking faces, and handle before use.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Select the correct weight and head material.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Use only compatible instruments.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Ensure the target instrument is appropriately positioned and supported.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Direct blows accurately and use the minimum force needed.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Maintain awareness of adjacent anatomical structures.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Replace instruments with damaged, loose, cracked, or deformed components.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "mallet_subsection_11",
          "subsection_title": "10. Precautions and contraindications",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "mallet_group_13",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "There are few instrument-specific absolute contraindications, but a mallet should not be used indiscriminately."
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Avoid forceful impact when bone quality or anatomy makes fracture likely.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Do not use a mallet with a loose head or damaged striking face.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Do not strike an instrument unless it is designed to receive impact.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Avoid uncontrolled blows near critical neurovascular structures.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Follow the surgeon's technique and the instrument manufacturer's instructions.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Use a powered or alternative instrument when manual impact is inappropriate.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "mallet_subsection_12",
          "subsection_title": "11. Care, cleaning and sterilization",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "mallet_group_14",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "title": "For reusable surgical mallets:",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Remove gross contamination promptly after use.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "1."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Clean the head, striking faces, handle, and any junctions thoroughly.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "2."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Follow the manufacturer's instructions for compatible detergents and cleaning methods.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "3."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Rinse and dry thoroughly.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "4."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Inspect the striking surfaces for pitting, cracks, deformation, or material loss.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "5."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Check that the head is securely attached and the handle is intact.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "6."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Sterilize using a validated method appropriate to the instrument's materials.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "7."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "High-yield point: Polymer- or nylon-faced mallets may have specific inspection and reprocessing requirements. Follow the manufacturer's instructions rather than assuming every mallet can be processed identically."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "mallet_subsection_13",
          "subsection_title": "12. High-yield OSCE viva questions",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "mallet_group_15",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q1. What is a surgical mallet?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A hand-held instrument used to deliver controlled impact force to another surgical instrument."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q2. What is the main principle of operation?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Transfer of kinetic energy through impact."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q3. Name three instruments that may be struck with a surgical mallet."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "An osteotome, a bone chisel, and a compatible bone punch."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q4. Does the mallet usually cut bone directly?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "No. It generally delivers impact to a cutting or shaping instrument, which acts on the bone."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q5. What materials may be used for the head?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Metal, nylon, polymer, or combinations of materials, depending on the intended use."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q6. Mention four complications."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Unintended fracture, soft-tissue injury, neurovascular injury, and damage to the receiving instrument."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q7. What should be checked before use?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The integrity of the head, security of its attachment, condition of the striking faces, and handle integrity."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q8. What is the difference between a mallet and a hammer?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A surgical mallet is designed for specific surgical applications and compatible instruments; the term hammer more broadly describes impact tools and may refer to specialized surgical hammers as well."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q9. What is an osteotomy?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A surgical procedure in which bone is deliberately cut or divided to correct alignment, reshape bone, or facilitate reconstruction."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q10. Why should damaged striking surfaces not be used?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "They may transmit force unpredictably and may release metal or polymer fragments."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
